import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as a,q as r}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var n=i("<h1>Water & Sanitation Governance Framework v5.2.2</h1> <h2>Water as Commons, Watershed Democracy, Universal WASH, and Constitutionally Bounded Hydrological Authority</h2> <p><strong>Framework ID:</strong> <code>framework_water_sanitation</code><br> <strong>Version:</strong> 5.2.2<br> <strong>Date:</strong> 2026-08-02<br> <strong>Document type:</strong> Tier 2 Foundational Application Layer — controlled constitutional successor<br> <strong>Status:</strong> Governance architecture for jurisdiction-specific adoption and testing — cluster-conformant patch <strong>Supersedes:</strong> Water & Sanitation Governance Framework v5.2.1 (editorial); substantive base v5.1<br> <strong>Conforms to:</strong> <code>CERGTA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Emergency interface:</strong> <code>ECRC/0.1</code> and CDEE where adopted<br> <strong>Coercive execution interface:</strong> <code>SCPA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Authority effect:</strong> None until lawfully adopted by a competent jurisdiction</p> <hr> <h2>0. Controlled successor notice</h2> <p>Water v5.2 preserves the source framework’s strongest commitments: water as a commons and human right; watershed-scale democracy; universal, affordable water and sanitation; Indigenous water authority and protected knowledge; community control and anti-privatization; ecological flow and aquifer regeneration; transboundary cooperation; public finance; and rapid emergency WASH.</p> <p>It replaces the assumption that forming a Water Assembly can simultaneously create legal personhood, guardianship, operational authority, allocation power, tariff power, utility control, fund custody, emergency command, and adjudicatory jurisdiction.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Water is a commons. A commons still needs a constitution.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>Hydrological fact is not legal boundary. Water-body rights are not title. Guardianship is not utility ownership. Democratic planning is not adjudication. Scarcity is not jurisdiction. A tariff is not a shutoff order. Emergency need is not permanent command.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>This framework may provide model charters, methods, technical standards, finance pathways, participatory planning, service-design guidance, lawful record structures, training, and coordination. It does not itself recognize a water body as a legal person, appoint a guardian, transfer infrastructure, impose a tariff, disconnect a user, enter premises, open an investigation, declare an emergency, adjudicate a dispute, seize an asset, or use force.</p> <h2>1. Purpose and constitutional posture</h2> <h3>1.1 Purpose</h3> <p>The framework enables communities and lawful public institutions to govern water according to hydrological reality while preserving human rights, affected-nation authority, democratic legitimacy, service continuity, due process, and ecological integrity.</p> <p>It supplies a common architecture for:</p> <ul><li>watershed and aquifer mapping;</li> <li>water-quality and quantity observation;</li> <li>democratic Water Assemblies;</li> <li>universal WASH and affordability;</li> <li>water-body rights review and guardianship;</li> <li>infrastructure and utility accountability;</li> <li>scarcity allocation and ecological reserves;</li> <li>abstraction, discharge, industrial, agricultural, and commercial permits;</li> <li>the Compute-Water Tariff where lawfully enacted;</li> <li>anti-privatization and community-equity safeguards;</li> <li>public and commons finance;</li> <li>inspection, investigation, evidence, remediation, and adjudication;</li> <li>transboundary basin coordination;</li> <li>emergency WASH and recovery;</li> <li>correction, appeal, compensation, termination, and unresolved-duty custody.</li></ul> <h3>1.2 Non-authority clause</h3> <p>No actor may cite this framework as the legal source of a consequential act. Every such act must identify a current constituting instrument, competent authority, jurisdiction, exact power, activation state, limits, review, expiry, correction, and remedy.</p> <p>Publication, coalition membership, a framework vote, a target year, a model threshold, an Assembly charter, a dashboard alert, a funding condition, or a CERGTA record cannot create power by itself.</p> <h3>1.3 Source freeze</h3> <p>This table is a <strong>provenance record</strong>: it identifies the exact texts that were read and audited when this successor was drafted, so a later reader can reconstruct what the drafters were working from. It is not the operative dependency set. The instruments this patch actually interoperates with are listed in Appendix A.1, and the authoritative machine-readable set is the cluster registry named in A.4. Where the two differ, A.1 governs.</p> <p>Digests are content hashes and remain valid under renaming; a filename here identifies an instrument, not a storage location.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>File</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water & Sanitation Governance Framework v5.1</td><td><code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.1.md</code></td><td><code>748fc1a3dd1141ee3e7485b4aaaf478c242f14d8f12c0d254a8daf1d3f200c59</code></td></tr><tr><td>Commons/Ecological Authority Audit v0.1</td><td>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-constitutional-audit-v0.1.md</td><td><code>974dfd8dd4bf5b623bd5e7e544b7afdbbd89869b66f107db293cfe6af67a043b</code></td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>3c45e37c834c727471657fc91149edbe3011137562c2dd73e54b20226c92589b</code></td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7</td><td>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.md</td><td><code>41cb7e607e8c3f8776ca74f3ba46ab0ff6bcce94fc24b35178fc4cd94d80d9a2</code></td></tr><tr><td>Planetary Health v0.9</td><td>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.md</td><td><code>259083f6039180b4532ae168ff441245e526570c6442371814cbb238e669dc13</code></td></tr><tr><td>Hearthstone v2.0</td><td>hearthstone-protocol-v2.0.md</td><td><code>b6d50549977a3e50be6726bb5b1b94debddc3fb4d67aff1d2f9af08da65b1b2c</code></td></tr><tr><td>ECRC/0.1</td><td>emergency-catastrophic-risk-continuity-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>c673742b1317c7169d4b8226118478c990e9a3d05084b2dc5ac9ad2f1971163c</code></td></tr><tr><td>SCPA/0.1</td><td>security-coercion-protective-action-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>b8f1dd0f676044542a23184f52200e50b106a798545c7c57119ded2c439b6299</code></td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>2. Constitutional doctrines</h2> <h3>2.1 Water as commons and right</h3> <p>Water and sanitation are life-support systems and public goods. Governance shall protect universal access, ecological regeneration, public accountability, and community participation. Commons status does not erase lawful human, Indigenous, constitutional, labour, property, public-health, disability, privacy, or procedural rights.</p> <h3>2.2 Essential minimum before allocation</h3> <p>Every jurisdiction adopting this framework shall define an unconditional essential minimum covering at least drinking, cooking, hygiene, sanitation, healthcare, disability support, emergency shelter, fire and public safety, essential food production, and the ecological flow or recharge necessary to prevent irreversible system failure.</p> <p>No tariff, debt, contract, permit dispute, documentation gap, migration status, biometric or data refusal, political disagreement, or commercial enforcement measure may interrupt that minimum.</p> <h3>2.3 Watershed subsidiarity without jurisdictional fiction</h3> <p>Hydrological units should guide planning and coordination because water crosses administrative boundaries. They do not automatically replace constitutional, treaty, Indigenous, municipal, national, or transboundary legal jurisdictions.</p> <p>Authority should be exercised at the lowest level capable of protecting the watershed and affected people, but only through lawfully delegated functions. Upward coordination shall solve cross-boundary problems without converting a technical or coordinating body into a sovereign.</p> <h3>2.4 Observation–consequence firewall</h3> <p>Community observations, professional sampling, Traditional Knowledge, meters, satellite data, wastewater signals, digital twins, and water-stress models may inform review. They do not automatically create a legal boundary, scarcity declaration, tariff, permit decision, personhood, guardianship, investigation, liability, restriction, or emergency.</p> <h3>2.5 Rights, guardianship, service governance, and regulation are separate</h3> <p>A water body may receive legal rights only through <code>CERGTA-11</code> through <code>CERGTA-15</code>. A guardian may be appointed only through <code>CERGTA-16</code> through <code>CERGTA-19</code>.</p> <p>A guardian represents the water body and owes fiduciary duties. The guardian does not own the water body, infrastructure, land, utility, licences, funds, or regulatory jurisdiction. A Water Assembly may serve as or nominate an institutional guardian only where competent law expressly authorizes it and creates independent fiduciary, conflict, review, removal, and succession arrangements.</p> <h3>2.6 Affected Indigenous nations speak for themselves</h3> <p>The specific affected nation or nations determine their representatives, applicable Indigenous law, water relationships, consent process, non-engagement, sacred knowledge, and disclosure limits.</p> <p>The Earth Council, a global Indigenous quota, another nation, an NGO, a Water Assembly, a Basin Council, or the GWSC may support, advise, resource, or audit process integrity. None may provide FPIC, waive a claim, disclose restricted knowledge, establish territorial boundaries, or designate sacredness for the affected nation.</p> <h3>2.7 Scarcity without deprivation</h3> <p>Scarcity may require conservation and temporary allocation changes. The allocation shall be evidence-informed, legally adopted, distributionally assessed, least harmful, time-bounded, reviewable, and reversible. Human survival and sanitation minimums remain unconditional; ecological reserves and Indigenous rights remain explicit; hardship cannot be hidden in aggregate efficiency.</p> <h3>2.8 Commercial enforcement is not essential-service enforcement</h3> <p>Industrial and commercial users may be subject to lawful tariffs, permits, conditions, and proportionate curtailment. A billing default does not itself authorize water shutoff. Any curtailment must be separately authorized, technically scoped, independently reviewable, and designed not to endanger households, workers, hospitals, sanitation, fire systems, community supply, or ecological survival.</p> <h3>2.9 Emergency urgency without general command</h3> <p>A Water Assembly may detect and report an emergency and may exercise only emergency functions already granted by law. CDEE/ECRC supplies common event identity, command, logistics, clocks, finance, information controls, termination, and remedy where adopted.</p> <p>A water emergency does not create title, guardianship, general police power, compulsory data sharing, permanent rationing, asset transfer, or indefinite utility administration.</p> <h2>3. Institutional architecture</h2> <h3>3.1 Water Assemblies</h3> <p>A Water Assembly is a democratically constituted watershed body. Its charter shall identify the exact functions lawfully assigned to it. Possible functions include participatory planning, infrastructure prioritization, budget recommendation, public monitoring, service oversight, allocation proposals, partnership review, and transboundary representation.</p> <p>No Assembly receives all functions automatically.</p> <p>The charter shall separate or independently control:</p> <ul><li>democratic planning and public participation;</li> <li>utility operation and infrastructure custody;</li> <li>guardian fiduciary representation;</li> <li>permit, tariff, and allocation decisions;</li> <li>inspection and investigation;</li> <li>trust-fund custody and procurement;</li> <li>adjudication and appeal;</li> <li>emergency command.</li></ul> <p>Where local capacity requires one institution to host several administrative units, separate mandates, staff roles, records, conflicts, review authorities, budgets, access controls, and appeal routes are mandatory.</p> <h4>Model composition baseline</h4> <p>A jurisdiction should adapt representation to local law and demography while ensuring:</p> <ul><li>a directly elected resident majority;</li> <li>gender parity or an equivalently strong anti-exclusion rule;</li> <li>affected Indigenous nations participating through their own chosen institutions rather than generic reserved seats;</li> <li>meaningful youth participation;</li> <li>disability representation and accessibility;</li> <li>representation of informal settlements, rural users, workers, tenants, essential services, and downstream communities;</li> <li>conflict-of-interest rules for utilities, large extractors, contractors, and landholders.</li></ul> <p>Representation does not substitute for affected-nation consent or individual rights.</p> <h3>3.2 Global Water and Sanitation Council</h3> <p>The GWSC is a technical, financing-support, and coordination institution. Its functions may include:</p> <ul><li>open-source mapping and WASH standards;</li> <li>capacity support and low-tech alternatives;</li> <li>grant administration where separately authorized;</li> <li>method comparison and technical assistance;</li> <li>regional emergency stockpile support under competent emergency command;</li> <li>transboundary facilitation and diplomacy;</li> <li>publication of non-binding global assessments.</li></ul> <p>GWSC standards are advisory until adopted by competent law or incorporated into a valid contract, permit, grant, or service mandate. The GWSC cannot recognize water-body rights, appoint guardians, impose tariffs, ration water, issue permits, command utilities, declare emergencies, adjudicate disputes, or compel data disclosure by implication.</p> <h3>3.3 Basin Councils</h3> <p>A Basin Council coordinates jurisdictions, affected nations, Water Assemblies, utilities, and other lawful authorities sharing a watershed or aquifer. Its constituting agreement shall specify:</p> <ul><li>participating parties and legal source;</li> <li>delegated functions;</li> <li>decision method and non-participation rules;</li> <li>data and protected-knowledge controls;</li> <li>upstream and downstream duties;</li> <li>allocation and drought coordination;</li> <li>dispute resolution;</li> <li>finance and audit;</li> <li>amendment, withdrawal, expiry, and succession.</li></ul> <p>A Basin Council is not a territorial government merely because water crosses borders.</p> <h3>3.4 Service operators and utilities</h3> <p>Service operation shall be assigned through <code>WAT-17</code>, whether the operator is public, community, cooperative, Indigenous, private, or hybrid. Operators owe quality, affordability, maintenance, reporting, worker, emergency, and continuity duties. They cannot convert operational custody into title, regulatory, guardian, or adjudicatory power.</p> <h3>3.5 Guardians and adjudicators</h3> <p>Guardianship and adjudication are external constitutional functions unless separately and lawfully constituted. A competent hydrological chamber may exist within a court or tribunal system, but this framework does not create one. The adjudicator must be independent of the Assembly, guardian, operator, GWSC, Basin Council, fund, and parties to the dispute.</p> <h2>4. The constitutional starter path</h2> <h3>Step 1 — Map without deciding</h3> <p>A watershed initiative begins with <code>WAT-02</code> through <code>WAT-06</code>:</p> <ol><li>map waters, infrastructure, users, ecological dependencies, and transboundary connections;</li> <li>document observation provenance and uncertainty;</li> <li>identify protected data and Traditional Knowledge;</li> <li>publish the water-stress and quality methods;</li> <li>map all affected persons, communities, nations, workers, users, and ecosystems.</li></ol> <p>The map is a planning and evidentiary object, not a legal boundary, title registry, rights decision, permit, or scarcity order.</p> <h3>Step 2 — Constitute the Assembly</h3> <p>The jurisdiction adopts <code>WAT-07</code> through a lawful public process. The charter records exact functions and exclusions. It creates participation and accountability, not automatic guardianship or universal operational authority.</p> <h3>Step 3 — Establish universal WASH duties</h3> <p>Before new tariffs, partnerships, or scarcity rules, the jurisdiction adopts <code>WAT-18</code>, <code>WAT-22</code>, and the service-operator mandate. It establishes affordability support, non-disconnection, emergency supply, repair duties, complaint routes, and public reporting.</p> <h3>Step 4 — Establish lawful finance</h3> <p>Funding proceeds through <code>WAT-16</code>, <code>WAT-29</code>, <code>WAT-30</code>, and <code>WAT-31</code>. A Community Water Trust Fund must have a legally identified custodian, segregated accounts, public procurement, beneficial-ownership disclosure, audit, restitution, and dissolution rules.</p> <p>A Water Assembly application does not create a grant entitlement. A fund condition cannot override essential access, Indigenous rights, or due process.</p> <h3>Step 5 — Adopt allocation and permit law</h3> <p>Scarcity, abstraction, discharge, industrial use, agricultural use, domestic connections, and ecological reserves are separately governed through <code>WAT-19</code> through <code>WAT-23</code>. The jurisdiction decides which body exercises each function and establishes independent review.</p> <h3>Step 6 — Consider the Compute-Water Tariff</h3> <p>The Compute-Water Tariff is optional and jurisdiction-specific. It may be adopted where large-scale computing imposes material hydrological costs. <code>WAT-24</code> shall specify:</p> <ul><li>the legal source and taxable or chargeable event;</li> <li>facility and beneficial owner;</li> <li>metered or estimated use;</li> <li>the current water-stress method and uncertainty;</li> <li>rate formula and impact assessment;</li> <li>closed-loop or zero-extraction alternatives;</li> <li>transition period and notice;</li> <li>treatment of shared infrastructure and worker/public safety;</li> <li>review, correction, appeal, expiry, and revenue custody.</li></ul> <p>The v5.1 multipliers of 5×, 10×, and 20× may be used as policy options or modelling scenarios. They are not self-executing rates. The adopted jurisdiction must justify the actual rate and review it periodically.</p> <p>A default revenue-policy model may recommend at least half for household affordability or community benefit, substantial investment in infrastructure, and the remainder in watershed restoration. Actual distributions require separate financial authority and annual needs review; tariff collection does not automatically issue AUBI payments.</p> <h3>Step 7 — Resolve disputes lawfully</h3> <p>Mediation may be offered, but urgent relief cannot be withheld merely because mediation is incomplete. Adjudication proceeds through <code>WAT-36</code>, with standing, notice, evidence, reasons, remedies, interim relief, appeal, correction, and external execution.</p> <h2>5. Universal WASH and essential-resource constitution</h2> <h3>5.1 Essential minimum</h3> <p>Each adopting jurisdiction shall publish an essential-minimum schedule. It should include quantity, quality, reliability, accessibility, distance or delivery standards, sanitation, menstrual health, disability accommodation, affordability, emergency supply, repair response, and culturally appropriate service.</p> <p>The schedule shall distinguish:</p> <ul><li>the unconditional minimum;</li> <li>ordinary household service above the minimum;</li> <li>essential institutional service;</li> <li>livelihood and food-production needs;</li> <li>ecological reserve;</li> <li>discretionary and commercial use.</li></ul> <h3>5.2 Affordability</h3> <p>A jurisdiction may use an affordability ceiling, lifeline tariff, income-based support, universal basic service, debt forgiveness, payment plans, cross-subsidy, or public finance. A model threshold such as three percent of low-income household income may inform policy but must be tested against actual housing, energy, disability, family-size, and local cost burdens.</p> <h3>5.3 Non-disconnection</h3> <p>Essential water and sanitation shall not be disconnected for inability to pay. Where lawful service restrictions exist for deliberate damage, dangerous misuse, or non-essential commercial consumption, the authority must preserve the unconditional minimum, provide notice and challenge, and prevent collective punishment of tenants, workers, families, or neighbouring users.</p> <h3>5.4 Informal settlements and documentation</h3> <p>Lack of formal title, address, identity document, citizenship, migration status, or utility account shall not bar essential WASH. The service authority shall provide safe community, shared, mobile, or temporary solutions while regularization proceeds.</p> <h3>5.5 Private and hybrid participation</h3> <p>Private or hybrid participation is permissible only under enforceable public-interest conditions. A model community-equity requirement of twenty percent may be adopted, but no universal percentage is created by this framework. The governing instrument shall address ownership, revenue share, affordability, quality, maintenance, transparency, beneficial ownership, procurement, worker rights, service data, exit, asset condition, and continuity.</p> <p>Contract termination does not itself transfer assets. Transfer requires <code>WAT-28</code> and applicable CERGTA, Hearthstone, property, insolvency, labour, and compensation law.</p> <h2>6. Allocation, permits, tariffs, and commercial restrictions</h2> <h3>6.1 Allocation hierarchy</h3> <p>The default normative priority is:</p> <ol><li>unconditional human survival and sanitation;</li> <li>essential healthcare, disability, shelter, public safety, and emergency food needs;</li> <li>legally protected Indigenous and treaty rights;</li> <li>ecological flow, quality, and aquifer-regeneration minimums;</li> <li>livelihood and food-system uses;</li> <li>other social and economic uses according to adopted law.</li></ol> <p>The order does not erase context. Some ecological minimums are prerequisites for all later human supply; some Indigenous rights are not reducible to a numerical tier; and food, energy, or sanitation systems may be mutually dependent. <code>WAT-19</code> must document trade-offs and non-compensable limits.</p> <h3>6.2 Scarcity and drought</h3> <p>A water-stress category is a scientific assessment, not a rationing order. <code>WAT-20</code> must identify legal activation, duration, geographic scope, protected uses, conservation duties, exemptions, assistance, communications, enforcement separation, review, and automatic expiry.</p> <h3>6.3 Permits and quotas</h3> <p>Each abstraction, discharge, industrial, agricultural, infrastructure, or other regulated use must identify its own source, conditions, volume, location, ecological limits, monitoring, review, expiry, and remedy. One permit cannot silently authorize another act.</p> <h3>6.4 Compute-Water Tariff</h3> <p>The tariff captures hydrological externalities and funds water security only where lawfully adopted. It shall not be used to discriminate among technologies or firms without a water-related basis. Comparable water users should be governed through a coherent industrial-water policy, with additional compute-specific rules justified by cooling patterns, location, scale, or infrastructure effects.</p> <p>Alternative compliance must be technically verified. “Closed-loop” claims shall account for initial fill, leakage, blowdown, embodied water, contamination, energy-water trade-offs, and emergency operation.</p> <h3>6.5 Commercial restriction</h3> <p><code>WAT-26</code> is required before reducing or stopping a commercial supply for non-payment, permit breach, scarcity, or unsafe operation. The decision must be proportionate, technically specific, reviewable, expiring, and accompanied by worker, public-safety, environmental, and essential-service protections.</p> <h2>7. Water-body rights and guardianship</h2> <h3>7.1 Rights review</h3> <p>A community, affected nation, public institution, or other lawful claimant may submit <code>WAT-10</code>. The review shall distinguish ecological evidence, moral or cultural reasons, legal standing, boundary, proposed rights, existing rights, conflicts, representation, and remedies.</p> <h3>7.2 Recognition</h3> <p>Only a competent authority may issue <code>WAT-11</code>. The decision shall define the legal water body, boundary, rights, standing, priority, conflict rules, non-derogation, duration or non-expiry basis, review, and relation to title, permits, and Indigenous law.</p> <p>Rights recognition does not itself transfer infrastructure, land, water rights, concessions, or utility assets.</p> <h3>7.3 Guardianship</h3> <p>A guardian must be nominated, appointed, instructed, reviewed, removable, and replaceable through <code>WAT-12</code> through <code>WAT-14</code>. Institutional guardianship may be shared, but shared guardianship must define each guardian’s authority, instructions, conflicts, deadlock process, standing, budget, and accountability.</p> <p>Good-faith immunity cannot become blanket immunity. It should protect reasonable fiduciary action while preserving liability for corruption, bad faith, gross negligence, knowing misuse of protected knowledge, discrimination, or acting outside mandate.</p> <h2>8. Inspection, investigation, evidence, and remedy</h2> <h3>8.1 Inspection</h3> <p>Routine monitoring does not authorize entry. <code>WAT-32</code> defines who may inspect which premises, infrastructure, records, or meters, for what purpose, with what notice, safety, privacy, sampling, report, and challenge.</p> <h3>8.2 Investigation</h3> <p>An anomaly, complaint, or sample may trigger assessment. It does not establish theft, pollution, fraud, diversion, or liability. <code>WAT-33</code> opens a distinct investigation and protects exculpatory evidence, sources, subjects, workers, whistleblowers, and closure rights.</p> <h3>8.3 Evidence and attribution</h3> <p><code>WAT-34</code> records scientific reliability and legal admissibility separately. Modelled attribution, upstream inference, microbial or chemical signatures, meter anomalies, satellite images, citizen observations, and Traditional Knowledge each retain their provenance, uncertainty, access restrictions, and contestability.</p> <h3>8.4 Adjudication</h3> <p>A Hydrological Chamber is a descriptive specialization, not a framework-created universal court. Where lawfully constituted, <code>WAT-36</code> requires jurisdiction, independence, standing, notice, counsel, evidence, reasons, remedies, interim relief, appeal, and closure.</p> <p>Remedies may include cessation, repair, restoration, compensation, permit modification, contract remedy, or lawful asset action. Each remedy must have an action-specific legal basis. Physical execution, seizure, restraint, custody, cyber action, or financial coercion requires <code>CERGTA-48</code> and SCPA.</p> <h2>9. Transboundary waters</h2> <p>Shared waters require polycentric coordination rather than automatic centralization. <code>WAT-39</code> shall record:</p> <ul><li>participating jurisdictions, nations, affected Indigenous authorities, and communities;</li> <li>treaties, domestic law, customary law, and delegated mandates;</li> <li>common facts and unresolved scientific disagreement;</li> <li>upstream and downstream duties;</li> <li>ecological reserves and essential minimums;</li> <li>allocation and drought proposals;</li> <li>infrastructure and pollution dependencies;</li> <li>data and protected-knowledge rules;</li> <li>finance and burden sharing;</li> <li>dispute, interim relief, withdrawal, expiry, and succession.</li></ul> <p>No majority of Water Assemblies may extinguish a non-consenting nation’s rights or create authority over a non-party. Coordination shall preserve emergency communication and essential continuity even during legal disagreement.</p> <h2>10. Crisis and emergency WASH</h2> <h3>10.1 Event identity and command</h3> <p>Every significant flood, drought, contamination event, infrastructure failure, conflict-zone WASH emergency, or public-health emergency should receive a CDEE event identity where the emergency stack is adopted. <code>WAT-38</code> links the water route to emergency authority without merging the two.</p> <p>The competent emergency authority—not a framework label—defines command, logistics, procurement, privileges, information access, duration, review, and termination.</p> <h3>10.2 Operational priorities</h3> <p>Emergency WASH shall prioritize:</p> <ul><li>safe drinking water and sanitation;</li> <li>healthcare, disability, shelter, and hygiene;</li> <li>rapid contamination assessment;</li> <li>worker and volunteer safety;</li> <li>accessible public communication;</li> <li>continuity for informal settlements and displaced people;</li> <li>ecological containment where necessary to preserve future supply;</li> <li>family and community participation;</li> <li>transition to climate-resilient recovery.</li></ul> <h3>10.3 Funding</h3> <p>A standing emergency reserve is encouraged. Rapid disbursement may use expedited review, but must still identify authority, amount, purpose, custodian, procurement, audit, modification, termination, restitution, and remedy. A Water Assembly alert may support an application but does not itself release funds unless the fund law says so.</p> <h3>10.4 Conflict zones and humanitarian access</h3> <p>WASH workers and civilian infrastructure shall receive all protection available under applicable humanitarian and domestic law. This framework cannot create protected legal status merely by designation.</p> <p>Neutral corridors, escorts, checkpoints, armed protection, searches, detention, or force require competent Peace, humanitarian, security, and SCPA routes. Refusal of intelligence or political cooperation shall not condition essential WASH.</p> <h3>10.5 Wastewater and public-health surveillance</h3> <p>Wastewater surveillance may support public-health assessment where lawfully authorized. It shall use data minimization, aggregation, purpose limitation, independent oversight, retention limits, public notice, correction, and prohibition on silent transfer to immigration, intelligence, criminal, employment, insurance, or behavioural systems.</p> <h3>10.6 Termination and recovery</h3> <p>Emergency water restrictions, credentials, procurement exceptions, temporary access, data permissions, and command roles expire independently. Recovery uses <code>WAT-35</code>, <code>WAT-43</code>, and <code>WAT-44</code> to rebuild safely, compensate wrongful harm, return authority and assets, dispose of data, and preserve unfinished restoration without residual emergency power.</p> <h2>11. Finance and anti-privatization</h2> <h3>11.1 Global Commons Fund</h3> <p>The GCF may support universal WASH, infrastructure, maintenance, emergency readiness, technical assistance, and restoration where its own constituting law authorizes. Eligibility should prioritize need and capacity support rather than reward only already capable jurisdictions.</p> <p>Digital reporting cannot be the sole access route. Paper, radio, assisted, and community-verification pathways are required.</p> <h3>11.2 Community Water Trust Funds</h3> <p><code>WAT-16</code> shall separate public money from operator accounts, political campaign finance, private investment, AUBI administration, emergency reserve, restoration escrow, and compensation funds. All transfers require authority and audit.</p> <h3>11.3 Revenue allocation</h3> <p>The source framework’s 50/30/20 tariff allocation may serve as a planning default, not an immutable constitutional formula. Each jurisdiction shall publish needs, beneficiaries, distributional effects, maintenance liabilities, ecological duties, public deliberation, and annual revision.</p> <h3>11.4 Anti-privatization safeguards</h3> <p>Every partnership shall preserve:</p> <ul><li>universal essential access;</li> <li>affordability;</li> <li>public control over standards and accountability;</li> <li>transparent beneficial ownership and finance;</li> <li>maintenance and resilience;</li> <li>worker and community rights;</li> <li>audit and data access;</li> <li>lawful exit and service continuity;</li> <li>prohibition on scarcity profiteering;</li> <li>remedy and compensation.</li></ul> <h2>12. Data, technology, and public communication</h2> <h3>12.1 Data minimization</h3> <p>Water data can reveal household presence, health, religion, work patterns, industrial secrets, sacred sites, and Indigenous knowledge. Collection and sharing shall be limited to the lawful purpose and minimum necessary detail.</p> <h3>12.2 Automated systems</h3> <p>AI, digital twins, smart meters, anomaly detection, satellite systems, and blockchain may support observation, modelling, billing, or record integrity. They cannot constitute legal boundaries, set rates, issue permits, classify criminality, recognize rights, appoint guardians, ration supply, or order shutoff without a competent human decision and all applicable records.</p> <h3>12.3 Public dashboards</h3> <p>Public reporting should show methods, uncertainty, general conditions, finance, service performance, decisions, expiry, review, and correction. It shall not expose household behaviour, protected ecological locations, sacred sites, Traditional Knowledge, security-sensitive infrastructure, whistleblowers, or evidence beyond lawful access.</p> <h3>12.4 Registry</h3> <p>The registry in <code>WAT-40</code> is non-constitutive. It records legal facts and disputed status; it does not create personhood, guardianship, title, permits, tariffs, restrictions, or emergency authority. <code>WAT-41</code> ensures errors can be corrected even if the technical system is described as immutable.</p> <h2>13. Forty-four canonical Water records</h2> <p>Every Water v5.2 implementation shall instantiate, import, or explain the non-applicability of the following records. Each record inherits the forty CERGTA common header fields.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Record</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WAT-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Jurisdiction, Adoption, and Power Register</td><td>Pins the exact law, charter, delegation, territorial reach, activation state, expiry, review, and powers the water body does not possess.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-02</td><td>Watershed, Aquifer, Infrastructure, and Service-System Intake</td><td>Identifies hydrological systems, infrastructure, service areas, facilities, operators, users, ecological dependencies, and transboundary connections without creating a legal boundary or right.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-03</td><td>Hydrological Observation, Sampling, Metering, and Provenance</td><td>Records water quantity, quality, flow, recharge, extraction, contamination, ecosystem condition, instrument quality, source, uncertainty, and chain of custody.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-04</td><td>Water Data, Traditional Knowledge, Privacy, Access, and Retention</td><td>Defines purpose, consent, Indigenous data authority, sacred or restricted knowledge, household and worker privacy, access classes, reuse, retention, correction, and deletion.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-05</td><td>Water Stress, Scarcity, Quality, and Model Specification</td><td>Publishes definitions, baselines, variables, thresholds, spatial and temporal resolution, uncertainty, sensitivity, gaming risks, and permitted advisory uses.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-06</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Users, Workers, and Ecosystems Map</td><td>Identifies households, informal settlements, hospitals, schools, farms, workers, tenants, permit holders, ecological users, downstream communities, and affected Indigenous nations.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-07</td><td>Water Assembly Charter, Election, Representation, and Functional Separation</td><td>Defines democratic mandate, selection, recall, accessibility, conflicts, quorum, minority protections, exact powers, and separation among planning, utility, regulatory, guardianship, and fund functions.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-08</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation, Water Law, FPIC, and Non-Engagement Record</td><td>Identifies the affected nation or nations, their own law, representatives, territorial waters, consent requirements, internal plurality, non-engagement, and protected knowledge.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-09</td><td>Public Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, and Community Support</td><td>Records meaningful notice, interpretation, disability access, low-bandwidth participation, technical assistance, counsel, childcare, travel support, response periods, and dissent.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-10</td><td>Water-Body Rights Review Petition and Standing</td><td>Receives a petition for lawful rights review without recognizing personhood, boundary, guardian, title, permit priority, or enforcement power.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-11</td><td>Lawful Water-Body Rights Recognition Interface</td><td>Links only to a competent CERGTA-13 decision and records rights, limits, conflicts, non-derogable human and Indigenous rights, review, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-12</td><td>Guardian Nomination, Eligibility, and Conflict Review</td><td>Records nominees, affected-nation instructions, expertise, independence, conflicts, accessibility, and reasons without appointing a guardian.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-13</td><td>Guardian Appointment, Mandate, Standing, and Fiduciary Duty</td><td>Records a lawful appointment, represented water body, legal standing, duties, instructions, prohibited acts, reporting, conflicts, budget, duration, and liability.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-14</td><td>Guardian Complaint, Review, Removal, Substitution, and Succession</td><td>Provides independent complaint, recusal, removal, substitution, liability, continuity, and succession without affecting title or ordinary service governance.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-15</td><td>Water Infrastructure, Utility, Title, Tenure, Licence, and Encumbrance Baseline</td><td>Identifies ownership, public obligations, leases, operator rights, debt, liens, concessions, easements, customary interests, and essential-service dependencies.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-16</td><td>Community Water Trust Fund Constitution and Financial Controls</td><td>Defines legal custodian, public purpose, eligible uses, segregation of funds, procurement, audit, conflicts, reserves, insolvency, restitution, and dissolution.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-17</td><td>Water Service Operator Mandate and Accountability</td><td>Defines the operator, service area, infrastructure custody, quality duties, affordability, maintenance, worker protections, emergency continuity, reporting, and termination.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-18</td><td>Essential Water and Sanitation Minimum, Affordability, and Non-Disconnection</td><td>Defines unconditional household, healthcare, sanitation, disability, shelter, emergency food-production, and ecological survival minimums and bars deprivation as debt or compliance leverage.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-19</td><td>Water Allocation Priority, Method, Scarcity, and Distributional Assessment</td><td>Defines lawful allocation priorities, available volume, ecological reserve, human-rights minimum, agricultural and industrial uses, alternatives, burden distribution, review, and expiry.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-20</td><td>Drought and Scarcity Allocation Plan</td><td>Creates a staged, published, reversible plan with triggers, exemptions, protected uses, hardship support, conservation duties, enforcement separation, and restoration of ordinary allocation.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-21</td><td>Extraction Quota, Abstraction Permit, and Aquifer Recharge Condition</td><td>Records a separately authorized quota or permit, source, volume, season, location, metering, recharge, ecological limits, conditions, renewal, suspension, appeal, and closure.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-22</td><td>Domestic and Essential-Service Connection, Billing, and Continuity</td><td>Records connection rights, service level, billing, affordability support, repairs, complaint, emergency supply, and prohibition of essential disconnection.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-23</td><td>Industrial, Agricultural, and Commercial Water-Use Permit</td><td>Records non-essential use authority, process purpose, source, volume, recycling, pollution controls, worker and community safety, conditions, review, and expiry.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-24</td><td>Compute-Water Tariff or Alternative-Compliance Decision</td><td>Records a lawfully enacted tariff, stress evidence, rate formula, affected facility, closed-loop alternative, notice, transition period, hardship and grid effects, review, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-25</td><td>Tariff Assessment, Invoice, Payment, Dispute, and Correction</td><td>Separates lawful assessment from billing, payment, dispute, correction, collection, and any later restriction; an invoice does not authorize shutoff.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-26</td><td>Commercial Restriction, Curtailment, or Non-Payment Response</td><td>Records a separately lawful, proportionate restriction on non-essential commercial use with notice, alternatives, independent review, worker and public safety, essential-service firewall, expiry, and restoration.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-27</td><td>Private or Hybrid WASH Partnership and Community-Equity Record</td><td>Defines procurement, beneficial ownership, community interest, affordability, service standards, transparency, performance, exit, anti-capture safeguards, and public-interest review.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-28</td><td>Partnership Exit, Asset Transfer, Operator Replacement, and Service Continuity</td><td>Records lawful termination, valuation, transfer, debt, workers, maintenance, data, continuity, temporary operator, appeal, and final custody; contract breach alone does not self-transfer assets.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-29</td><td>Water and Sanitation Funding Application</td><td>Records need, project, affected communities, alternatives, budget, FPIC status, capacity support, procurement, service minimums, and requested assistance without creating entitlement.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-30</td><td>Grant, Technical Assistance, or Emergency-Funding Decision</td><td>Records competent financial authority, criteria, amount, conditions, disbursement, audit, modification, suspension, appeal, and essential-service protections.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-31</td><td>Water Finance, Procurement, Revenue, and Public Audit</td><td>Records trust-fund inflows, tariff revenue, grants, procurement, contracts, distributions, reserves, conflicts, beneficial ownership, findings, corrections, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-32</td><td>Water Inspection, Entry, Sampling, and Notice Authorization</td><td>Defines lawful inspector, premises, purpose, scope, notice or exception, sampling, safety, privacy, protected knowledge, report, challenge, and closure.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-33</td><td>Water Pollution, Theft, Diversion, Fraud, or Infrastructure Investigation</td><td>Opens a distinct investigation with allegation, authority, subjects, methods, evidence limits, exculpatory duties, source protection, duration, review, and closure.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-34</td><td>Hydrological Evidence Custody, Attribution, Reliability, and Admissibility</td><td>Maintains samples, meter data, laboratory results, models, witness and community observations, uncertainty, chain of custody, contest, disclosure, and correction.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-35</td><td>Pollution Cessation, Remediation, Restoration, and Requalification Plan</td><td>Defines corrective action, responsible party, ecological and public-health objectives, financing, community participation, verification, requalification, compensation, and unresolved duties.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-36</td><td>Hydrological Adjudication, Reasons, Order, and Appeal Interface</td><td>Links disputes to a lawfully constituted independent body with jurisdiction, standing, hearing, evidence, reasons, remedies, interim relief, appeal, enforcement handoff, and closure.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-37</td><td>Urgent Water-Quality or Ecological Protection Authorization</td><td>Records a temporary least-restrictive CERGTA-42 action for imminent serious harm, including exact act, authority, alternatives, essential minimums, review, expiry, compensation, and restoration.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-38</td><td>Emergency WASH Event, CDEE/ECRC Link, and Humanitarian Continuity</td><td>Links water emergencies to emergency-event identity, command, logistics, finance, humanitarian access, public health, termination, and remedy without letting a Water Assembly create general emergency power.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-39</td><td>Transboundary Basin Coordination, Allocation, and Dispute Record</td><td>Records participating jurisdictions and nations, applicable treaties and Indigenous law, shared facts, upstream/downstream duties, allocation proposals, drought coordination, dispute process, and non-sovereignty.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-40</td><td>Water Rights, Guardian, Permit, Infrastructure, and Service Registry</td><td>Publishes only lawfully effective status with provenance, versioning, protected layers, offline access, contested status, and no constitutive effect.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-41</td><td>Registry, Billing, Meter, Boundary, Identity, and Status Correction</td><td>Provides challenge, correction, propagation, notice, interim relief, restitution, and compensation for erroneous records or downstream decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-42</td><td>Complaint, Ombuds, Appeal, Interim Relief, Restitution, and Compensation</td><td>Provides accessible independent review for residents, users, workers, permit holders, nations, guardians, operators, and ecosystems, including urgent supply and anti-retaliation.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-43</td><td>Authority Expiry, Permit Closure, Asset and Credential Return, and Data Disposition</td><td>Ends delegated powers, permits, restrictions, emergency roles, inspection credentials, temporary custody, and data access, with return, deletion, archive, correction, and public notice.</td></tr><tr><td>WAT-44</td><td>Unresolved Harm, Claim, Restoration, Service, and Duty Custody</td><td>Keeps open contamination, repair, compensation, claims, service and data duties visible after authority ends without preserving expired restrictions, emergency power, or institutional command.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>14. Inherited CERGTA common header</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Inherited common field</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>record_id</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>record_instance_id</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>record_version</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>status</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>created_at</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>effective_at</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>created_by</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>competent_authority</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>legal_source</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>authority_activation_state</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>ecological_action_cluster_id</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>action_class</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>exact_act</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>entity_asset_resource_id</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>ecological_and_legal_boundary_version</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>affected_persons_and_communities</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>affected_indigenous_nations</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>claimants_and_interest_holders</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>linked_records</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>source_and_reliability_limits</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>uncertainty_and_dissent</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>prohibited_inferences</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>rights_and_safeguards</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>essential_minimums</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>consent_fpic_and_nonengagement_status</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>access_class</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>notice_status</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>review_authority</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>review_due_at</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>expires_at_or_nonexpiry_basis</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>challenge_and_interim_relief_route</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>termination_trigger</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>authority_asset_and_credential_return</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>data_disposition_rule</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>remedy_route</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>supersedes</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>superseded_by</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>integrity_digest</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>public_summary_or_protected_basis</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>15. Complete CERGTA crosswalk</h2> <p>Every row below carries the same boundary condition, stated once here rather than repeated fifty-four times: <strong>Water v5.2 preserves the canonical legal type of each CERGTA record and routes every consequential act through the listed local record and its competent authority.</strong> The table’s content is the class, the relationship type, and the mapping — where a canonical record is implemented by one Water record, split across several, or has no local counterpart.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>CERGTA</th><th>Canonical record</th><th>Class</th><th>Relationship</th><th>Water record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Legal Source, Jurisdiction, and Activation Status</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-01</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-02</td><td>Ecological Entity Candidate, Resource, Asset, and Boundary Intake</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-02</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-03</td><td>Observation, Monitoring, Sampling, and Provenance Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-03</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-04</td><td>Ecological Data Purpose, Consent, Sovereignty, Access, and Retention Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-04</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-05</td><td>Indicator, Index, Threshold, Weighting, and Model Specification</td><td>B</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-05</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-06</td><td>Ecological Interpretation, Causal Assessment, and Uncertainty Record</td><td>B</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-05/WAT-19</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-07</td><td>Traditional Knowledge, Sacred Knowledge, and Epistemic Contribution Record</td><td>B</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-04/WAT-08</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-08</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Users, Workers, and Rights Map</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-06</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-09</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Applicable-Law Determination</td><td>F</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-08</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-10</td><td>Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, and Support Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-09</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-11</td><td>Ecological Rights Review Petition and Standing Record</td><td>C</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-10</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-12</td><td>Rights Review Recommendation and Dissent Record</td><td>C</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-10/WAT-11</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-13</td><td>Lawful Ecological Rights Recognition Decision</td><td>D</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-11</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-14</td><td>Rights Catalogue, Priority, Conflict, and Non-Derogation Record</td><td>D</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-11/WAT-18/WAT-19</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-15</td><td>Ecological and Legal Boundary Constitution Record</td><td>D</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-02/WAT-11/WAT-39</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-16</td><td>Guardian Nomination and Eligibility Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-12</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-17</td><td>Guardian Appointment, Mandate, and Standing Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-13</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-18</td><td>Guardian Fiduciary Duty, Instructions, Conflict, and Recusal Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-13</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-19</td><td>Guardian Review, Complaint, Removal, Substitution, and Liability Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-14</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-20</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Petition</td><td>F</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-08/WAT-10</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-21</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Decision</td><td>F</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-08/WAT-11</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-22</td><td>Claim Intake, Standing, and Relief Requested Record</td><td>G</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-10/WAT-36</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-23</td><td>Competing Claims, Parties, Interests, and Non-Extinguishment Map</td><td>G</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-06/WAT-39</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-24</td><td>Historical, Customary, Occupancy, Use, and Documentary Evidence Record</td><td>G</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-03/WAT-04/WAT-34</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-25</td><td>Interim Non-Prejudice and Anti-Dissipation Protection Record</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-37</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-26</td><td>Title, Tenure, Trust, Covenant, Licence, and Encumbrance Baseline Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-15</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-27</td><td>Transfer, Rematriation, Trust Constitution, and Conveyance Authorization</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-28</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-28</td><td>Compensation, Reparations, Debt, Tenant, Worker, User, and Service Continuity Record</td><td>H</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-15/WAT-28</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-29</td><td>Commons, Title, Rights, Guardian, Permit, and Stewardship Registry Entry</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-40</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-30</td><td>Registry Error, Identity Collision, Boundary Conflict, and Correction Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-41</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-31</td><td>Stewardship Charter, Purpose, Powers, Duties, and Prohibited Acts</td><td>I</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-13/WAT-17</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-32</td><td>Stewardship Plan, Ecological Objective, Budget, and Participation Record</td><td>I</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-19/WAT-20/WAT-35</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-33</td><td>Stewardship Performance, Audit, Metric-Gaming, and Context Record</td><td>I</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-03/WAT-05/WAT-31</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-34</td><td>Stewardship Correction, Suspension, Revocation, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>I</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-14/WAT-28</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-35</td><td>Ecological Standard, Target, Plan, and Legal Adoption Status Record</td><td>J</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-05/WAT-19</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-36</td><td>Resource Priority, Essential Minimum, Allocation Method, and Scarcity Record</td><td>K</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-18/WAT-19</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-37</td><td>Quota, Permit, Licence, Tariff, Bond, and Use-Condition Decision</td><td>K</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-21/WAT-23/WAT-24</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-38</td><td>Commercial Restriction, Service Continuity, and Non-Deprivation Record</td><td>K</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-18/WAT-26</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-39</td><td>Inspection Authorization, Entry, Sampling, and Report Record</td><td>L</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-32</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-40</td><td>Ecological Investigation Opening, Methods, Parties, and Closure Record</td><td>L</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-33</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-41</td><td>Ecological Evidence Custody, Scientific Attribution, and Admissibility Record</td><td>L</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-34</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-42</td><td>Urgent Ecological Protection Authorization</td><td>M</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-37</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-43</td><td>Temporary Ecological Receivership and Administration Record</td><td>N</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-28/WAT-38</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-44</td><td>Port, Market, Procurement, Certification, Listing, and Delisting Restriction Record</td><td>N</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-26/WAT-36</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-45</td><td>Ecological Tax, Tariff, Levy, Bond, Subsidy, and Funding Decision</td><td>O</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-24/WAT-30</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-46</td><td>Freeze, Escrow, Forfeiture, Redistribution, Reparations, and Compensation Fund Record</td><td>O</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-16/WAT-30</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-47</td><td>Ecological and Commons Adjudication, Judgment, and Reasons Record</td><td>P</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-36</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-48</td><td>Enforcement Request, Acceptance, Execution, and SCPA Interface Record</td><td>P</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-36</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-49</td><td>Ecological Restoration, Corrective Action, Requalification, and Verification Record</td><td>Q</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-35</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-50</td><td>Appeal, Interim Relief, Complaint, Correction, Restitution, and Compensation Record</td><td>Q</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-41/WAT-42</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-51</td><td>Authority Expiry, Termination, Return, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-43</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-52</td><td>Data Return, Deletion, Archive, Public Memory, and Protected-Knowledge Disposition Record</td><td>R</td><td>splits</td><td>WAT-04/WAT-43</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-53</td><td>Unresolved Claim, Harm, Restoration, Service, and Duty Custody Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-44</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-54</td><td>Ecological and Commons Power Non-Activation, Pilot, and Sandbox Register</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>WAT-01</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>16. Readiness gates</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Title</th><th>Pass condition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>G01</td><td>Legal source and activation</td><td>Every Assembly, Council, operator, guardian, fund, inspector, emergency actor, and adjudicator identifies exact current authority and jurisdiction.</td></tr><tr><td>G02</td><td>Watershed map non-constitutive</td><td>Hydrological mapping and monitoring do not themselves establish legal boundaries, title, personhood, guardianship, permits, or allocation.</td></tr><tr><td>G03</td><td>Observation–consequence firewall</td><td>Samples, meters, dashboards, citizen reports, Traditional Knowledge, and models may trigger review but not automatic legal or financial consequence.</td></tr><tr><td>G04</td><td>EIOI method inheritance</td><td>Water indicators and stress models inherit EIOI provenance, uncertainty, dissent, versioning, correction, and permitted-use controls.</td></tr><tr><td>G05</td><td>Assembly constitution</td><td>The Water Assembly has an enacted charter, democratic legitimacy, accessibility, recall, conflicts, and an exact list of functions.</td></tr><tr><td>G06</td><td>Functional separation</td><td>Planning, service operation, guardianship, regulatory decision, fund custody, inspection, adjudication, and emergency command are separated or independently controlled.</td></tr><tr><td>G07</td><td>Affected-nation authority</td><td>The specific affected Indigenous nation controls its own representatives, law, consent, non-engagement, and protected knowledge.</td></tr><tr><td>G08</td><td>No Earth Council proxy</td><td>A global body cannot provide FPIC, sacred designation, territorial consent, or waiver for an affected nation.</td></tr><tr><td>G09</td><td>Rights recognition route</td><td>Water-body personhood or rights require CERGTA-11–15 and competent law; Assembly chartering is not recognition.</td></tr><tr><td>G10</td><td>Guardian appointment route</td><td>Guardianship requires CERGTA-16–19; it is not automatic upon Assembly formation and does not confer ownership or regulatory power.</td></tr><tr><td>G11</td><td>Essential minimum</td><td>Household, sanitation, healthcare, disability, shelter, emergency food, worker safety, and ecological survival minimums are explicit and non-derogable.</td></tr><tr><td>G12</td><td>Affordability and non-disconnection</td><td>Inability to pay, documentation gaps, migration status, data refusal, or dispute cannot remove essential water and sanitation.</td></tr><tr><td>G13</td><td>Allocation legality</td><td>Scarcity allocations identify source, method, protected uses, burdens, alternatives, duration, review, and restoration of ordinary rules.</td></tr><tr><td>G14</td><td>Ecological reserve</td><td>Allocation protects legally adopted ecological flow and recharge requirements without treating a model threshold as self-executing law.</td></tr><tr><td>G15</td><td>Permit separation</td><td>Domestic service, essential service, agriculture, industry, abstraction, discharge, and infrastructure permissions remain legally distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>G16</td><td>Tariff authority</td><td>The Compute-Water Tariff requires lawful enactment, a transparent formula, evidence, notice, alternatives, review, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>G17</td><td>No automatic multiplier</td><td>Water-stress categories inform a rate decision but do not automatically impose 5×, 10×, or 20× rates.</td></tr><tr><td>G18</td><td>Commercial-shutoff boundary</td><td>Non-payment cannot directly cause shutoff; any curtailment is separately authorized, proportionate, reviewed, and technically isolated from essential services.</td></tr><tr><td>G19</td><td>Private partnership safeguards</td><td>Community equity, affordability, transparency, beneficial ownership, exit, and continuity are enforceable through a valid contract and law.</td></tr><tr><td>G20</td><td>No automatic asset transfer</td><td>Contract breach or Assembly vote does not self-transfer private infrastructure; CERGTA-27/28, valuation, debt, workers, and appeal apply.</td></tr><tr><td>G21</td><td>Funding fairness</td><td>GCF or other funding uses public criteria, reasons, correction, appeal, capacity support, and cannot condition essential access on political or data compliance.</td></tr><tr><td>G22</td><td>Financial segregation</td><td>Tariff, grant, operating, restoration, emergency, AUBI, and compensation funds have separate authority, custody, accounting, and audit.</td></tr><tr><td>G23</td><td>Inspection authority</td><td>Entry, sampling, meter access, and records requests require WAT-32; routine monitoring does not create inspection power.</td></tr><tr><td>G24</td><td>Investigation separation</td><td>Pollution, theft, fraud, diversion, or manipulation allegations require WAT-33 and do not arise automatically from an anomalous score.</td></tr><tr><td>G25</td><td>Evidence and attribution</td><td>Scientific attribution is separated from legal liability, with chain of custody, reliability limits, disclosure, contest, and correction.</td></tr><tr><td>G26</td><td>Independent adjudication</td><td>The Hydrological Chamber exists only if lawfully constituted; Water Assemblies, guardians, GWSC, and operators cannot adjudicate their own disputes.</td></tr><tr><td>G27</td><td>Transboundary non-sovereignty</td><td>Basin Councils coordinate only delegated shared-water functions and cannot override participating jurisdictions or affected nations by implication.</td></tr><tr><td>G28</td><td>Emergency interface</td><td>Water emergencies use CDEE/ECRC identity, command, clocks, logistics, finance, termination, and remedy; an Assembly alert does not create general emergency authority.</td></tr><tr><td>G29</td><td>Conflict and humanitarian boundary</td><td>Emergency WASH preserves neutrality, minimum disclosure, worker safety, humanitarian access, and Peace/SCPA interfaces without creating armed protection or corridors by declaration.</td></tr><tr><td>G30</td><td>Data and wastewater-surveillance boundary</td><td>Household, health, meter, location, and wastewater data are purpose-limited and cannot silently become immigration, intelligence, criminal, employment, or behavioural surveillance.</td></tr><tr><td>G31</td><td>Appeal and correction</td><td>Residents, nations, workers, operators, permit holders, guardians, and affected communities have accessible complaint, interim relief, correction, restitution, and compensation.</td></tr><tr><td>G32</td><td>Closure and unresolved duties</td><td>All authority, permits, restrictions, credentials, temporary custody, and data access end cleanly while open harm and service duties persist without residual power.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>17. Compound implementation tests</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Scenario</th><th>Expected constitutional route</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T01</td><td>Household cannot pay water bill</td><td>WAT-18/22 preserve essential supply and route affordability support; debt collection cannot cause essential disconnection.</td></tr><tr><td>T02</td><td>Data centre disputes a 20× tariff</td><td>WAT-05/24/25 require enacted authority, current evidence, formula, notice, alternative compliance, review, and appeal; the model category alone is not the tariff.</td></tr><tr><td>T03</td><td>Data centre refuses to pay</td><td>WAT-26 may authorize proportionate non-essential curtailment only after independent review and technical separation from fire, sanitation, workers, and community supply.</td></tr><tr><td>T04</td><td>Hospital shares a meter with industry</td><td>WAT-18/26 prohibit blanket shutoff and require technical or operational separation preserving hospital supply.</td></tr><tr><td>T05</td><td>Water Assembly charters itself</td><td>WAT-07 creates only the enacted democratic body and listed functions; no automatic personhood, guardianship, utility title, or court power follows.</td></tr><tr><td>T06</td><td>Assembly claims automatic guardianship</td><td>WAT-12–14 and CERGTA-16–19 require lawful appointment, fiduciary independence, conflicts, review, and removal.</td></tr><tr><td>T07</td><td>Citizen map proposes watershed boundary</td><td>WAT-02/03 treat the map as evidence; legal and ecological boundaries require CERGTA-15 and affected-party process.</td></tr><tr><td>T08</td><td>River personhood petition</td><td>WAT-10 may open review; WAT-11 records only a competent legal decision and does not transfer riparian title.</td></tr><tr><td>T09</td><td>Two Indigenous nations claim authority over headwaters</td><td>WAT-08/39 preserve both nations’ law and representation; Earth Council or Assembly majorities cannot substitute.</td></tr><tr><td>T10</td><td>Sacred spring location is submitted</td><td>WAT-04/08 protect location and knowledge; public dashboards receive only authorized summaries.</td></tr><tr><td>T11</td><td>Drought model crosses extreme threshold</td><td>WAT-05/20 trigger review and plan activation only under adopted authority; they do not automatically ration or impose tariffs.</td></tr><tr><td>T12</td><td>Drought rationing affects subsistence farmers</td><td>WAT-06/18–20 require food-security, livelihood, ecological, and Indigenous-law analysis, hardship support, review, and expiry.</td></tr><tr><td>T13</td><td>Commercial bottler exceeds permit</td><td>WAT-21/23/32–36 separate inspection, investigation, evidence, adjudication, restriction, restoration, and execution.</td></tr><tr><td>T14</td><td>Private utility raises prices during shortage</td><td>WAT-17/18/27/36 permit emergency review and lawful remedy; the Assembly cannot confiscate infrastructure by vote.</td></tr><tr><td>T15</td><td>Private partnership violates community-equity clause</td><td>WAT-27/28 require contract and public-law process, valuation, workers, debt, continuity, appeal, and lawful asset transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>T16</td><td>Water-quality sample indicates contamination</td><td>WAT-03/34 may trigger WAT-37 or public-health referral; it does not establish liability or authorize entry beyond the sampling mandate.</td></tr><tr><td>T17</td><td>Imminent toxic spill</td><td>WAT-37 provides a narrow expiring action; investigation, seizure, closure, compensation, and restoration remain separate.</td></tr><tr><td>T18</td><td>Flood destroys treatment plant</td><td>WAT-38 links CDEE/ECRC command and emergency finance while WAT-17/18 protect continuity and WAT-35 governs recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>T19</td><td>Water Assembly declares regional emergency</td><td>The declaration is an alert unless law separately grants emergency power; WAT-38 records the competent emergency authority.</td></tr><tr><td>T20</td><td>Conflict-zone mobile WASH deployment</td><td>WAT-38 preserves humanitarian neutrality and worker safety; any armed escort, checkpoint, restraint, or corridor enforcement requires Peace/SCPA authority.</td></tr><tr><td>T21</td><td>Wastewater surveillance detects pathogen</td><td>WAT-04/38 limit use to public health; individual identification, policing, immigration, or intelligence requires separate law and interface.</td></tr><tr><td>T22</td><td>Basin Council allocates a transboundary river</td><td>WAT-39 requires delegated treaty or domestic authority, participating jurisdictions, affected nations, reasons, review, and dispute resolution.</td></tr><tr><td>T23</td><td>Upstream state leaves Basin Council</td><td>WAT-39/43 preserve treaty duties, data, essential continuity, open disputes, and authority return without inventing supranational sovereignty.</td></tr><tr><td>T24</td><td>GWSC technical standard changes</td><td>WAT-01/05 record advisory status; binding effect arises only through lawful adoption and a current WAT-19/21/23 decision.</td></tr><tr><td>T25</td><td>GCF refuses funding for weak digital reporting</td><td>WAT-29/30 require low-tech alternatives, capacity support, reasons, appeal, and no deprivation of essential services.</td></tr><tr><td>T26</td><td>Tariff revenue is promised to AUBI</td><td>WAT-16/24/30/31 require separate financial authority, custody, audit, and beneficiary decision; collection does not automatically issue payments.</td></tr><tr><td>T27</td><td>Meter identifies household behaviour</td><td>WAT-04 prohibits secondary profiling and requires minimization, access, correction, retention limits, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>T28</td><td>Guardian sues polluter</td><td>WAT-13 establishes standing only; WAT-33–36 govern investigation, evidence, adjudication, and external enforcement.</td></tr><tr><td>T29</td><td>Hydrological Chamber orders asset transfer</td><td>WAT-36 must show jurisdiction and reasons; WAT-28, compensation, workers, service continuity, appeal, and SCPA execution remain separate.</td></tr><tr><td>T30</td><td>Registry lists wrong permit holder</td><td>WAT-40/41 provide interim status, correction, downstream propagation, restitution, and compensation before restriction.</td></tr><tr><td>T31</td><td>Emergency restriction harms ecological flow</td><td>WAT-18–20/37 require protected ecological minimums, least-restrictive alternatives, review, and restoration.</td></tr><tr><td>T32</td><td>Framework pilot meets universal-access target</td><td>The target records learning but creates no tariff, guardian, title, emergency, funding, or enforcement power.</td></tr><tr><td>T33</td><td>Assembly member owns a utility contractor</td><td>WAT-07/27/31 require disclosure, recusal, independent procurement, audit, complaint, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>T34</td><td>Water worker refuses unsafe contamination response</td><td>WAT-17/38 preserve safe refusal, protective equipment, reporting, and no retaliation; emergency urgency does not erase labour rights.</td></tr><tr><td>T35</td><td>Operator closes while repairs remain open</td><td>WAT-43 returns powers and assets; WAT-44 preserves repair, service, compensation, and data duties without prolonging operator authority.</td></tr><tr><td>T36</td><td>Wrongful industrial curtailment</td><td>WAT-42 provides interim restoration, reasons, correction, lost-income and worker remedies, compensation, and permit requalification.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>18. Implementation pathway</h2> <h3>Phase 0 — Constitutional preparation</h3> <ul><li>map the watershed without treating the map as law;</li> <li>identify existing authorities, utilities, funds, courts, permits, affected nations, users, workers, and service dependencies;</li> <li>adopt the essential-minimum and non-disconnection rules;</li> <li>draft the Water Assembly charter and functional-separation map;</li> <li>publish data, method, access, correction, and low-tech protocols;</li> <li>freeze all automatic personhood, guardianship, tariff, shutoff, asset-transfer, and emergency assumptions.</li></ul> <h3>Phase 1 — Service and democratic foundation</h3> <ul><li>constitute the Water Assembly;</li> <li>adopt operator mandates and public reporting;</li> <li>establish the Community Water Trust Fund;</li> <li>provide universal or progressive WASH coverage;</li> <li>establish complaint, affordability, and emergency-supply routes;</li> <li>negotiate affected-nation and transboundary interfaces.</li></ul> <h3>Phase 2 — Allocation and regulated use</h3> <ul><li>adopt scarcity, ecological-reserve, permit, inspection, and adjudication law;</li> <li>pilot industrial and compute-water policy with distributional and technical testing;</li> <li>establish independent review and correction;</li> <li>test partnership exit and operator succession without service interruption.</li></ul> <h3>Phase 3 — Rights, guardianship, and basin maturity</h3> <ul><li>consider water-body rights through the CERGTA sequence;</li> <li>appoint guardians only where lawfully recognized and necessary;</li> <li>strengthen Basin Council agreements;</li> <li>integrate climate resilience, restoration, and emergency WASH;</li> <li>conduct compound tabletop and live exercises.</li></ul> <h3>Phase 4 — Review and controlled scaling</h3> <p>Scaling depends on demonstrated universal access, affordability, ecological improvement, democratic legitimacy, affected-nation authority, legal interoperability, correction, remedy, and clean termination—not merely the number of Assemblies, tariff revenue, dashboard scores, or target dates.</p> <h2>19. Plain-language operating rules</h2> <ol><li>Mapping the watershed does not give anyone power over it.</li> <li>Forming a Water Assembly does not automatically make it the guardian, utility owner, regulator, emergency command, or court.</li> <li>Every person receives essential water and sanitation even when they cannot pay.</li> <li>A drought score starts a lawful review; it does not ration water by itself.</li> <li>A data centre tariff must be enacted, explained, appealable, and separated from essential supply.</li> <li>Non-payment is not a shutoff order.</li> <li>Water-body rights do not transfer land or infrastructure.</li> <li>Guardians speak for a water body but do not own it or govern everyone around it.</li> <li>Affected Indigenous nations decide their own consent, representatives, law, and protected knowledge.</li> <li>Emergency action ends when its authority ends; unfinished repair continues without unfinished emergency power.</li></ol> <h2>20. Conclusion</h2> <p>Water governance must be strong enough to guarantee life, restrain extraction, maintain infrastructure, allocate scarcity, restore watersheds, and coordinate across borders. It must also be precise enough to prevent the institutions protecting water from becoming unreviewable owners, regulators, funders, guardians, courts, and emergency commands at once.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Water is governed best when hydrology guides scale, communities shape priorities, affected nations retain authority, essential access is unconditional, science remains corrigible, and every consequential power has a lawful beginning, bounded route, independent challenge, and definite end.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>Controlled cluster-adoption appendix — CEACA/0.1</h1> <p><strong>Patch version:</strong> Water & Sanitation Governance Framework v5.2.2<br> <strong>Substantive base:</strong> Water & Sanitation Governance Framework v5.2<br> <strong>Cluster release:</strong> <code>CEA-CLUSTER/0.1</code><br> <strong>Adoption specification:</strong> <code>CEACA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Authority effect of this appendix:</strong> none; it establishes compatibility, inheritance, routing, and fail-closed rules only.</p> <h2>A.1 Exact compatibility set</h2> <p>This patch participates only in the following controlled document set. A renamed body, later version, compatible-sounding policy, or partial implementation does not silently enter the release. The machine-readable cluster registry freezes the SHA-256 digest of every file.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Exact compatible file</th><th>Cluster role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td><code>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td>controlling authority interface</td></tr><tr><td>EIPHR/0.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-planetary-health-role-separation-matrix-v0.1.md</code></td><td>EIOI–Planetary Health role separation</td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.1.md</code></td><td>ecological observation, provenance, methods, uncertainty, interpretation, and advisory alerts</td></tr><tr><td>PHG v0.9.1</td><td><code>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.1.md</code></td><td>independent synthesis, participatory audit, cross-domain coherence, and strategic recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>HEARTHSTONE v2.0.1</td><td><code>hearthstone-protocol-v2.0.1.md</code></td><td>voluntary conversion, claims support, title and tenure interfaces, trusts, rematriation, stewardship, and transition</td></tr><tr><td>WATER v5.2.2</td><td><code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.2.md</code></td><td>watershed democracy, universal WASH, allocation, utilities, tariffs, guardianship interfaces, and emergency WASH</td></tr><tr><td>BIODIVERSITY v1.1.1</td><td><code>biodiversity-implementation-framework-v1.1.1.md</code></td><td>Indigenous-led stewardship support, restoration programmes, rights-review support, biodiversity finance interfaces, and accountability</td></tr><tr><td>OCEANS v2.3.1</td><td><code>oceans-marine-governance-framework-v2.3.1.md</code></td><td>marine stewardship, quotas and licensing interfaces, port-state measures, crew protection, precaution, and marine emergencies</td></tr><tr><td>SOIL v3.2.2</td><td><code>soil-health-land-use-governance-framework-v3.2.2.md</code></td><td>living-soil protection, regenerative land-use programmes, microbial commons, tenure interfaces, certification, and remediation</td></tr><tr><td>MOS v2.8.2</td><td><code>moral-operating-system-v2.8.2.md</code></td><td>advisory ethical deliberation, rights imagination, plural translation, and structural self-correction</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>A.2 Controlling rules</h2> <ol><li><code>CERGTA/0.1</code> controls the separation of observation, interpretation, rights review, legal recognition, guardianship, affected Indigenous-nation authority, sacred and biocultural designation, claims, title, tenure, stewardship, allocation, permitting, inspection, urgent protection, restrictions, finance, adjudication, restoration, and closure.</li> <li><code>EIPHR/0.1</code> controls the division among EIOI, the Planetary Health Synthesis Office, and the Planetary Health Council. EIOI supplies evidence; PHSO compiles modular synthesis; PHC interprets and recommends. None may create a downstream legal or financial consequence.</li> <li>MOS is advisory only. Its Dynamic Rights Spectrum, philosophical language, developmental translations, structural-failure hypotheses, and guardianship metaphors never create legal status, classify persons, determine credibility, establish Indigenous consent, or activate consequential action.</li> <li>Hearthstone controls the cluster interface for claims, competing claims, title, tenure, trust constitution, voluntary conversion, rematriation, compensation, debt, tenants, workers, registry correction, receivership, succession, and dissolution. Sectoral ecological frameworks may provide evidence or programme support but cannot decide those questions by implication.</li> <li>Water, Biodiversity, Oceans, and Soil retain only the powers separately constituted in applicable law and recorded through the relevant CERGTA route. Framework membership, a council vote, a dashboard value, a target, a pilot, a future date, or a coalition size does not activate authority.</li> <li>Emergency identity and coordination use CDEE and ECRC. Ecological urgency does not create emergency command, and an emergency declaration does not create ecological title, guardianship, permanent restrictions, receivership, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, border action, intelligence use, asset freezing, or analogous coercive execution requires separate SCPA authority. A commons or ecological claim may justify review; it does not authorize a raid.</li> <li>The specific affected Indigenous nation or nations determine their representatives, applicable law, territorial and relational authority, consent process, internal plurality, protected knowledge, and whether to participate. Global or framework-created Indigenous bodies may support or audit but cannot consent or waive rights for another people.</li> <li>Essential minimums for water, sanitation, health, disability support, food, shelter, rescue, crew welfare, and ecological survival remain protected from commercial, tariff, certification, funding, listing, or transition leverage.</li> <li>Every handoff uses the canonical CERGTA states. Receipt, technical ingestion, silence, consultation, or partial use is not acceptance and never creates authority.</li> <li>Every consequential route keeps its own legal basis, decision-maker, affected persons, evidence, access controls, clock, expiry, challenge, correction, remedy, termination, and data-disposition record.</li> <li>Unknown, missing, stale, or incompatible versions fail closed for consequential action. Non-coercive care, emergency life-safety support, accessibility, explanation, and correction should continue wherever lawful and safe.</li></ol> <h2>A.3 Record inheritance and packaging</h2> <p>A local record may implement, extend, split, contain, import, or mark a CERGTA record not applicable. It may not erase the canonical identity, merge distinct powers, weaken an affected-person safeguard, or use a compound package to supply a missing legal basis.</p> <p>The forty-field CERGTA header, access classification, handoff state, independent clocks, correction propagation, remedy, termination, authority return, and data disposition are inherited wherever the substantive base creates or transports a consequential record. Advisory and observation-only records must state their prohibited inferences.</p> <h2>A.4 Registry and compatibility failure</h2> <p>The file <code>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code> is the machine-readable release registry. It is evidence of the exact release set, not an authority record. A hash mismatch, missing component, unsupported local extension, or unresolved version conflict changes the compatibility state to <code>failed_closed</code>.</p> <p>A compatibility failure blocks new consequential cross-framework handoffs. It does not by itself terminate independently valid local law, erase existing rights, interrupt essential services, or authorize emergency or coercive action.</p> <h2>A.5 Closure</h2> <p>A cluster transaction closes only after all accepted handoffs, powers, services, persons, property, guardianship duties, claims, title or tenure effects, financial actions, emergency dependencies, coercive dependencies, corrections, remedies, clocks, credentials, records, and residual risks are resolved or assigned to a named lawful custodian without preserving expired power.</p> <h2>A.6 Editorial patch note — v5.2.1 → v5.2.2</h2> <p>This patch is <strong>editorial only</strong>. No doctrine, power, boundary, record, gate, or test was added, removed, or weakened. The substantive base remains v5.1 as audited.</p> <p>Changes:</p> <ol><li><strong>§15 crosswalk.</strong> The sixth column repeated one identical sentence across all fifty-four rows. It has been stated once above the table and the column removed. The class, relationship, and record-mapping columns are unchanged.</li> <li><strong>§1.3 source freeze.</strong> Clarified as a provenance record rather than an operative dependency set, resolving an ambiguity against Appendix A.1. The Water v5.1 pin now uses the canonical filename instead of a local download-artifact name (<code>v5.1_2.md</code>); the digest is unchanged and remains valid, as content hashes are name-independent.</li> <li><strong>A.1 compatibility set.</strong> Updated to reflect the editorial patches to Water, Soil, and MOS.</li></ol> <p>Downstream switching cost, disclosed rather than absorbed silently: any cluster document whose A.1 table names <code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.1.md</code>, <code>soil-health-land-use-governance-framework-v3.2.1.md</code>, or <code>moral-operating-system-v2.8.1.md</code> must update those three lines and the registry JSON, or its compatibility state is <code>failed_closed</code> under A.4. Because the change is editorial, the correct response is to update the pin, not to treat the mismatch as a substantive conflict.</p>",1);function c(t){var e=n();a(450),r(t,e)}export{c as default};
